            <ArticleTitle>The Socioeconomic Impact of Morbid Obesity and Factors Affecting Access to Obesity Surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>669-79</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.suc.2016.03.002</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0039-6109(16)30002-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Bariatric surgery has been shown in many studies to be the most effective long-term treatment for severe obesity and obesity-related comorbidities. Economic analysis has demonstrated cost-effectiveness as well as cost-savings in select subgroups of patients. Despite the health and economic benefits of bariatric surgery, relatively few eligible patients receive this treatment. This disparity in access to care must be addressed by health policy decision-makers.</AbstractText>
